course: physics
lesson: 3
title: Parachutes, skydivers and falling safely
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The faster something falls and the bigger the side it turns to the air, the harder the air pushes back on it.
explain: When a parachute opens, its huge cloth catches so much air that the skydiver slows from racing-car speed to a gentle landing.
explain: Nature uses the same trick: a dandelion seed hangs under its fluffy parachute, and a sycamore seed spins down like a little helicopter.
explain: Landing safely is about stopping slowly: an egg dropped on a pillow survives, but on the hard floor it stops in an instant and cracks.
explain: That is why bike helmets have squashy foam, gymnasts land on mats and cars have airbags.
fact: A parachute's big cloth catches lots of air, and the push of the air grows with the area and the speed until it balances the skydiver's weight.
fact: Without a parachute a skydiver falls at about 200 kilometres an hour; with it open, the landing is about as hard as jumping off a low wall.
fact: Skydivers spread out like a starfish to catch more air, and dive head first like an arrow to go faster.
fact: A dandelion seed is tiny and light, and its hairs make a big area for its weight, so it falls very slowly and the wind carries it far.
fact: Scientists found that a dandelion seed's parachute makes a little ring of swirling air above it that holds it up longer.
fact: A sycamore seed's wing makes it spin, and the spinning slows its fall, giving the wind time to carry it away from its parent tree.
fact: A falling cat twists its front half one way and its back half the other to turn its feet down, but high falls still hurt cats.
fact: When a lift starts going down, you fall a little with it and press less on the floor, and your loose insides give a floating feeling; going fast over a hump-backed bridge in a car gives the same feeling.
fact: A quick stop needs a huge force, and a slow stop needs a much smaller one, which is why a pillow saves an egg.
fact: The foam in a bike helmet crushes in a crash and slows the head more gently, so a helmet that has had a big knock should be replaced.
fact: A penny dropped from a skyscraper is flat, light and tumbles, so air slows it to a speed that would sting but not seriously hurt.
fact: Heavy, pointed things dropped from a height really are dangerous, which is why building sites need hard hats.
fact: A lacy snowflake drifts down slowly, but a hailstone is a solid ball of ice and pelts down; big hailstones can dent cars.
exercise: Why does a skydiver slow right down when the parachute opens? | The huge cloth catches lots of air, which pushes up hard | The parachute is lighter than air | The parachute switches off gravity | The cloth pulls the skydiver up to the plane
exercise: Why does a bigger parachute fall more slowly? | A bigger area catches more air | A bigger one is lighter | Big cloth is pulled less by gravity | The wind cannot reach a small one
exercise: About how fast does a skydiver fall before the parachute opens? | About 200 kilometres an hour | About 20 kilometres an hour | About 2,000 kilometres an hour | As slow as a walking person
exercise: Why do skydivers spread out like a starfish? | To catch more air and slow the fall | To fall faster and faster to the ground | To keep their arms and legs warm up high | To steer straight back towards the plane
exercise: How is a dandelion seed like a parachute? | Its fluffy hairs catch air so it falls very slowly | It is heavier at the top than the bottom | It opens out a tiny cloth of its own just before landing | It is filled with helium gas
exercise: What does a sycamore seed do as it falls? | It spins like a helicopter and falls slowly | It drops straight down to the ground like a stone | It floats upwards on its own, away from the tree | It flaps its thin wing up and down like a bird
exercise: Why would a tree want its seeds to fall slowly? | The wind can carry them away from the parent tree | Slow seeds always grow into taller, stronger trees | Fast seeds would break the ground | Birds can only eat fast seeds
exercise: How does a cat turn over in the air with nothing to push on? | It twists its front half one way and its back half the other | It flaps its tail like a propeller | It pushes against the air with its paws | It cannot, cats always land on their backs
exercise: Why does your tummy feel funny when a lift starts going down? | You fall a little with the lift and press less on the floor | The lift squeezes the air out of you | Your stomach gets heavier and drops lower as the lift goes down | Gravity pulls harder in lifts
exercise: Why does an egg dropped on a pillow not break? | The pillow stops it slowly with a small force | The pillow makes the egg lighter as it lands on it | The pillow is wet and sticky | Eggs are stronger when they are soft
exercise: Why does a bike helmet have squashy foam inside? | It crushes and slows your head more gently | It keeps your head cool in summer | It makes the helmet light enough to wear all day | It holds the helmet on tightly
exercise: Why should you replace a helmet after a big crash, even if it looks fine? | Its foam is already crushed and cannot protect again | The plastic changes colour and fades | Helmets get heavier after a crash | It is the law to buy one each year
exercise: Why is a falling penny not as dangerous as people say? | Air slows the flat, tumbling coin to a gentle speed | Pennies are too light for gravity | Pennies always land in the wind | Pennies melt on the way down
exercise: Why do building sites need hard hats? | Heavy, pointed things falling from height are dangerous | Workers like to look the same | Hard hats keep the rain off | The sun is too bright there
exercise: What makes a snowflake fall so slowly? | It is light and lacy, so air holds it up a lot | It is colder than the air | Snow is pulled much less by gravity than rain is | It is pushed up by the clouds
exercise: Which of these works like a crash mat, a pillow and an airbag? | All of them make the stop slower and gentler | All of them make you bounce higher when you land | All of them make you lighter | All of them stop you at once
test: Is it true that a coin dropped from a really tall building could kill someone? | No, air slows it to a sting | Yes, it falls faster than a bullet | Yes, coins speed up for ever | No, coins never reach the ground
test: What would happen if a skydiver dived head first like an arrow? | They would fall much faster | They would fall more slowly | They would stop in the air | They would rise back up
test: bruh my cat fell off the shed and landed on its feet so cats can fall off anything right | No, high falls still hurt cats | Yes, cats never get hurt | Yes, cats float when they fall | No, cats always land on their backs
test: Which feeling is most like a lift starting to go down? | Going over a hump-backed bridge in a car | Standing very still in a long queue at school | Lying quietly in bed at night, half asleep | Walking slowly up a long flight of stairs
test: You must drop an egg from a window without breaking it. Which idea is best? | Land it on something soft that stops it slowly | Paint it so it is harder | Drop it faster so it is quick | Hold it by a string and let go
test: In a game a hero jumps off a cliff and lands on stone without a scratch. Why would a real jump hurt? | The hard stone stops the body in an instant | Gravity is stronger in real life | Real stone is sticky | Real people are heavier than heroes in games
test: Your helmet fell off the table and has a tiny scratch. When must a helmet be replaced? | After a big knock that crushes the foam | After every single small scratch or mark | Every month, no matter what has happened | Never, because good helmets last for ever
test: Why does hail dent cars but snow does not? | Hailstones are solid ice and fall much faster | Snow is warmer than hail | Hail is pulled much harder by gravity than snow | Snow is always wet and soft
test: Why does a car have an airbag? | It makes the stop of your body slower and gentler | It makes the car lighter in a crash | It keeps the air fresh | It pushes the car away from the other car in a crash
test: Without a parachute, why would a dandelion seed not travel far? | It would fall quickly near its parent | It would fly too high up to land anywhere | It would be much too heavy for the wind | It would start to grow while still in the air
test: Mate reckons a parachute works cos the cloth is lighter than air. right? | No, it works because air pushes on the big cloth | Yes, parachute cloth floats up | Yes, it is filled with light gas | No, it works because the cloth is heavy and steadies you
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the deployment of a parachute diminishes the velocity of descent. | The extensive canopy encounters a considerable resistance of the air | The canopy is of lesser density than the surrounding air | The canopy neutralises the gravitational attraction | The canopy draws the parachutist upwards towards the aircraft
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the foam lining of a cycling helmet? | It prolongs the arrest of the head and so diminishes the force | It reduces the weight of the head during a fall | It increases the rebound of the head from the ground | It prevents the helmet from being displaced by a strong wind when cycling
exam: It is given that a coin is released from the summit of a tall building. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning its descent. | The air restrains it to a speed insufficient to cause grave injury | It attains a velocity sufficient to prove fatal | It accelerates without limit until impact | It is borne upwards and does not descend
exam: Ascertain the advantage to a tree of seeds that descend slowly. | The wind may convey them to a distance from the parent | The seeds are rendered heavier upon landing | The seeds are protected from all animals | The seeds germinate in the air some time before landing
